  "query_preview": "..The center of a Pacific ridge has shifted southward\ntoward northern Mexico this morning, bringing a westerly flow\naloft to Utah and southwest Wyoming today. The flow will continue\nto increase today as a Pacific Northwest system moves over the top\nof this feature. With improved mixing, temperatures will be a bit\nwarmer than yesterday, with maxes averaging 5-10F above seasonal\nnormals.\n\nThe weakening trough is expected to track across northern Utah and\nsouthwest Wyoming tonight into Saturday morning. Precipitation\n(valley rain, snow above 6-7kft) will initially be focused near\nthe Utah/Idaho border, shifting south overnight into early\nSaturday. Accumulations will be light, no more than a few inches\nfor the mountains, but this may cause some brief travel\ndifficulties overnight. Most precipitation will be done Saturday\nmorning, with a few showers expected to linger over the northern\nmountains into the afternoon hours. \n\nHigh pressure will make its way back over the area for Sunday,\nbringing a day of dry conditions and temperatures on the mild\nside. This is expected to quickly shift east, however, as a rather\nweak trough grazes northern Utah for Monday, most likely bringing\njust a few showers near the Utah/Idaho border. Guidance continues\nto indicate the potential for a broad closed low to move across\nthe Desert Southwest for the latter half of the upcoming week,\nbringing the potential for cooler temperatures and some more\nsubstantial precipitation, at least for southern Utah.",
